1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,194
The textbook's answer is one sentence:
nobody's, as far as Git is concerned.

2
00:00:09,234 --> 00:00:13,181
A hook is a program that Git runs
at a fixed point of a command.

3
00:00:13,221 --> 00:00:17,168
A client-side hook is a file in
one clone that one option skips.

4
00:00:17,208 --> 00:00:22,339
Policy is enforced where every push arrives:
on the server and in CI, the automated

5
00:00:22,379 --> 00:00:22,775
checks.

6
00:00:23,115 --> 00:00:26,727
There's a second story in this
video, and it runs the other way.

7
00:00:26,767 --> 00:00:31,179
You download a research repository as a
tar file, unpack it, and type the most

8
00:00:31,219 --> 00:00:35,574
innocent command there is: git status.
A program written by the author of the

9
00:00:35,614 --> 00:00:38,541
archive runs on your machine,
with your permissions.

10
00:00:38,581 --> 00:00:41,338
How can looking be dangerous?
Hold that question.

11
00:00:41,678 --> 00:00:44,793
Both stories are the same
fact seen from two sides.

12
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:48,691
Hooks and configuration are local
and aren't copied by a clone.

13
00:00:48,731 --> 00:00:51,970
That's why you can't push
your hook onto a colleague.

14
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:56,795
It's also why a clone is safe to look at
and an unpacked .git directory isn't.

15
00:00:58,635 --> 00:01:02,416
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

16
00:01:02,456 --> 00:01:07,114
In the previous video you learned which
hooks run when, and you wrote five.

17
00:01:07,154 --> 00:01:12,312
You also saw three gaps: commands that
create commits without the commit hooks, the

18
00:01:12,352 --> 00:01:14,818
index-versus-disk trap, and --no-verify.

19
00:01:15,158 --> 00:01:20,078
Today: why hooks aren't cloned, the ways to
share them and what each one costs, what

20
00:01:20,118 --> 00:01:24,921
hooks and configuration mean for security,
and where a rule has to live if it must

21
00:01:24,961 --> 00:01:25,984
hold for everyone.

22
00:01:27,824 --> 00:01:29,850
explain why hooks are not cloned;

23
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:34,806
share hooks through core.hooksPath and
through hooks defined in configuration, and

24
00:01:34,846 --> 00:01:36,055
give the cost of each;

25
00:01:36,395 --> 00:01:38,678
run a hook by name with git hook run;

26
00:01:39,018 --> 00:01:43,264
show what an unpacked archive of a
repository can execute that a clone cannot;

27
00:01:43,604 --> 00:01:47,278
state what enforces policy when
a client-side hook does not.

28
00:01:49,118 --> 00:01:54,080
Why hooks are not cloned. git clone
transfers objects and refs: the stored

29
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:59,352
snapshots, and the names that point at
them. .git/hooks and .git/config aren't

30
00:01:59,392 --> 00:02:04,827
transferred, by design. Otherwise cloning
a repository would mean agreeing to run

31
00:02:04,867 --> 00:02:06,314
its author's programs.

32
00:02:06,654 --> 00:02:10,192
Sharing, first way: a tracked
directory and core.hooksPath.

33
00:02:10,232 --> 00:02:13,832
Keep the hooks in the repository
and tell Git to look there.

34
00:02:13,872 --> 00:02:18,684
The directory travels. The setting is
configuration, and each clone must make it

35
00:02:18,724 --> 00:02:23,536
once. The cost: from then on every pull
can change what runs on the next commit.

36
00:02:23,576 --> 00:02:27,539
Review changes under that directory
as carefully as changes to CI.

37
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:31,489
Sharing, second way: hooks
defined in configuration.

38
00:02:31,529 --> 00:02:36,893
Since Git 2.54 a hook can be declared
as hook.<name>.command plus one or more

39
00:02:36,933 --> 00:02:40,543
hook.<name>.event values,
in any configuration file.

40
00:02:40,583 --> 00:02:46,369
One script can serve several events and
several repositories, and several hooks can

41
00:02:46,409 --> 00:02:50,861
share an event. hook.<name>.enabled=false
switches one hook off.

42
00:02:51,201 --> 00:02:56,552
The textbook adds that hook.<name>.parallel
and hook.jobs allow parallel runs since

43
00:02:56,592 --> 00:03:01,618
Git 2.55, except for hooks such as
pre-commit and commit-msg that touch shared

44
00:03:01,658 --> 00:03:06,555
state. The cost: a hook defined in a
global configuration file runs in every

45
00:03:06,595 --> 00:03:11,556
repository of that user. That suits a
personal secret scanner, and equally an

46
00:03:11,596 --> 00:03:13,894
attacker who can write to that file.

47
00:03:14,234 --> 00:03:19,601
Other ways to distribute. init.templateDir
names a directory whose contents are

48
00:03:19,641 --> 00:03:24,254
copied into every new .git, hooks
included, on one person's machine.

49
00:03:24,294 --> 00:03:30,003
A setup script that copies files into
.git/hooks works everywhere and has to be run.

50
00:03:30,043 --> 00:03:32,262
Hook managers automate that step.

51
00:03:32,602 --> 00:03:37,365
--no-verify in full. With git commit
it skips pre-commit and commit-msg.

52
00:03:37,405 --> 00:03:42,235
With git merge, pre-merge-commit and
commit-msg. With git push, pre-push.

53
00:03:42,275 --> 00:03:47,305
With git rebase, pre-rebase. With git
am, applypatch-msg and pre-applypatch.

54
00:03:47,345 --> 00:03:49,640
No option skips a server-side hook.

55
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:54,486
Hook security. In one sentence: a hook,
and several configuration values, are

56
00:03:54,526 --> 00:03:59,209
commands that run with your permissions,
so the question "is it safe to use this

57
00:03:59,249 --> 00:04:03,283
repository" is the question "where
did its .git directory come from".

58
00:04:03,323 --> 00:04:06,471
The security section of the
git manual draws the line.

59
00:04:06,811 --> 00:04:11,785
Because configuration and hooks aren't
copied by git clone, it's generally safe to

60
00:04:11,825 --> 00:04:15,271
clone a repository with untrusted
content and inspect it.

61
00:04:15,311 --> 00:04:20,224
It isn't safe to run Git commands in a
.git directory, or the working tree around

62
00:04:20,264 --> 00:04:25,361
it, that itself came from an untrusted source
such as an archive or a shared folder.

63
00:04:25,701 --> 00:04:29,804
The rest of the threat model, in
the order you're likely to meet it.

64
00:04:29,844 --> 00:04:34,738
Hooks you chose to run: a tracked hooks
directory, an included configuration file

65
00:04:34,778 --> 00:04:39,673
and a hook manager turn "pull" into "update
the programs that run on my machine".

66
00:04:39,713 --> 00:04:44,730
Hooks in global configuration. Repositories
owned by someone else: Git refuses them

67
00:04:44,770 --> 00:04:46,740
unless safe.directory lists them.

68
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:52,821
Bugs that write into .git during a clone:
the textbook cites CVE-2024-32002, which

69
00:04:52,861 --> 00:04:58,673
got a hook written into a submodule's .git
and run while the clone was in progress.

70
00:04:58,713 --> 00:05:03,185
So keep Git current, and don't
clone untrusted repositories with

71
00:05:03,225 --> 00:05:08,826
--recurse-submodules. And a bare repository
inside a working tree, which a later

72
00:05:08,866 --> 00:05:09,813
chapter shows.

73
00:05:10,153 --> 00:05:14,778
Why hooks cannot enforce policy. A
client-side hook fails open in four ways.

74
00:05:14,818 --> 00:05:17,909
It isn't installed by
cloning. One option skips it.

75
00:05:17,949 --> 00:05:22,942
It doesn't run for commits made in the web
interface, through an API, or by a tool

76
00:05:22,982 --> 00:05:26,748
with its own Git implementation,
a coding agent's for example.

77
00:05:26,788 --> 00:05:31,598
And several Git commands that create
commits don't run the commit hooks at all.

78
00:05:31,938 --> 00:05:36,789
Enforcement needs a place that every change
must pass and that its author doesn't

79
00:05:36,829 --> 00:05:41,680
control. There are two. The server: on a
Git server that you run, the server-side

80
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:46,510
hooks pre-receive and update. And CI as
a required check: whatever a hook checks

81
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:51,461
locally, a workflow can check for every
pull request, and a ruleset, GitHub's list

82
00:05:51,501 --> 00:05:56,412
of rules, can require it before a merge.
The hook then saves a round trip, and the

83
00:05:56,452 --> 00:05:59,854
check is the control. Run the
same script in both places.

84
00:06:01,694 --> 00:06:06,060
Keep the tripwires from the last video.
You place them in your own workshop.

85
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:09,365
They do nothing in a colleague's.
You can step over them.

86
00:06:09,705 --> 00:06:14,789
Today add the door. Every delivery, from
every workshop, passes one door: the server

87
00:06:14,829 --> 00:06:18,511
that receives the push, and the
CI run that a merge requires.

88
00:06:18,551 --> 00:06:23,513
A check at the door doesn't care how the
delivery was packed or whether its sender

89
00:06:23,553 --> 00:06:24,367
has tripwires.

90
00:06:24,707 --> 00:06:29,057
For security, turn the picture around.
When you clone, you receive the goods:

91
00:06:29,097 --> 00:06:33,789
objects and refs. When you unpack someone's
archive that contains a .git directory,

92
00:06:33,829 --> 00:06:38,407
you move into their workshop, with their
tripwires already set, and the wires are

93
00:06:38,447 --> 00:06:40,687
connected to programs of their choosing.

94
00:06:41,027 --> 00:06:46,527
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds.
Write three layers: a client-side hook, a

95
00:06:46,567 --> 00:06:52,417
server-side check, and CI as a required
check. Mark every layer its author can skip.

96
00:06:52,457 --> 00:06:53,118
I'll wait.

97
00:06:59,458 --> 00:07:03,280
Only the first layer gets a mark.
The first layer is feedback.

98
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:08,201
The second and third are controls. The
same script can run in the first and the

99
00:07:08,241 --> 00:07:08,575
third.

100
00:07:10,415 --> 00:07:15,552
Your clone refuses the debug line. The rest
of the snippet shows Asha's clone of the

101
00:07:15,592 --> 00:07:19,004
same repository: it has no
hook, and her commit is made.

102
00:07:19,344 --> 00:07:24,524
The first way to share. 🟡 CAUTION: git
config set core.hooksPath decides which

103
00:07:24,564 --> 00:07:26,799
programs Git runs on your machine.

104
00:07:27,139 --> 00:07:31,053
Predict: after Asha's pull,
does the hook run in her clone?

105
00:07:31,093 --> 00:07:32,863
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

106
00:07:37,703 --> 00:07:41,404
Not yet. The directory arrived
with the pull. The setting didn't.

107
00:07:41,744 --> 00:07:46,825
Until she runs the git config set herself,
her clone still looks in .git/hooks.

108
00:07:46,865 --> 00:07:52,141
A relative core.hooksPath is relative to
the directory in which the hook runs, the

109
00:07:52,181 --> 00:07:53,696
top of the working tree.

110
00:07:54,036 --> 00:07:56,549
The second way: hooks as
configuration entries.

111
00:07:56,889 --> 00:08:02,273
Three hooks for one event now: a global
one, a local one named marker, and a hook

112
00:08:02,313 --> 00:08:03,545
file in .git/hooks.

113
00:08:03,885 --> 00:08:08,617
git hook list --show-scope is the first
diagnostic for "why did something run".

114
00:08:08,657 --> 00:08:13,448
It shows, in order of execution, the
configured hooks with the scope of the file

115
00:08:13,488 --> 00:08:17,072
that defined them, and last the
file in the hooks directory.

116
00:08:17,412 --> 00:08:22,322
One reassurance about that word global.
Here it's the lab's isolated global file,

117
00:08:22,362 --> 00:08:27,272
not your own. The lab is a sealed room.
Your real configuration stays outside it,

118
00:08:27,312 --> 00:08:29,717
and nothing typed in the lab reaches it.

119
00:08:30,057 --> 00:08:36,810
Now the switches. A quick quiz: does
core.hooksPath=/dev/null silence every hook, or

120
00:08:36,850 --> 00:08:40,207
only the hooks directory? Say it out loud.

121
00:08:45,047 --> 00:08:50,770
Only the hooks directory. If you said
every hook, the manual is on your side: it

122
00:08:50,810 --> 00:08:56,245
describes core.hooksPath=/dev/null as
a way to "disable all hooks entirely".

123
00:08:56,585 --> 00:09:02,222
On Git 2.55.0 it silenced the hooks
directory, and the configured hook still ran:

124
00:09:02,262 --> 00:09:08,040
"committing as" is printed. --no-verify
skipped the pre-commit hooks of both kinds.

125
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:13,434
git hook run executes the hooks of an event,
so CI and wrapper tools can run what a

126
00:09:13,474 --> 00:09:15,214
developer's commit would run.

127
00:09:15,554 --> 00:09:20,787
You unpack it and look around. Predict
what git status -s prints in a repository

128
00:09:20,827 --> 00:09:22,566
with no changes. I'll wait.

129
00:09:27,406 --> 00:09:31,869
git status, the command people run to
look around, executed a command from the

130
00:09:31,909 --> 00:09:35,911
archive. The switch ran the hook.
That's how looking can be dangerous.

131
00:09:36,251 --> 00:09:39,572
A configuration value that's
a command, and a hook file.

132
00:09:39,612 --> 00:09:41,133
Both came in the tar file.

133
00:09:41,473 --> 00:09:46,644
The manual's remedy is a clone, with
--no-local, so that objects are transferred

134
00:09:46,684 --> 00:09:50,032
through the normal protocol
and not copied as files.

135
00:09:50,372 --> 00:09:53,833
The clone has the history, without
the setting and without the hook.

136
00:09:53,873 --> 00:09:54,451
Nothing ran.

137
00:09:57,791 --> 00:10:00,788
The guard is bypassed: the
rule lives in one clone.

138
00:10:01,128 --> 00:10:07,415
Follow origin/main. It pointed at eafde45.
First, Asha's clone has no hook, so her

139
00:10:07,455 --> 00:10:12,971
unfinished commit 20aa35f went up.
Then --no-verify sent yours, 18e1b38.

140
00:10:13,311 --> 00:10:17,480
The lab then moves the rule into a
pre-receive hook in the bare repository, and

141
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:18,706
tries the bypass again.

142
00:10:27,046 --> 00:10:32,567
--no-verify made no difference. "[remote
rejected]" with "pre-receive hook declined"

143
00:10:32,607 --> 00:10:37,997
is how every client reports a server-side
refusal. A clone with no hooks at all is

144
00:10:38,037 --> 00:10:39,586
refused in the same way.

145
00:10:39,926 --> 00:10:45,407
This segment is described from the framework's
documentation, and nothing in it was

146
00:10:45,447 --> 00:10:50,928
run. A file .pre-commit-config.yaml in the
repository lists hook repositories, each

147
00:10:50,968 --> 00:10:56,383
pinned with rev and each providing hooks
by id. pre-commit install sets up the Git

148
00:10:56,423 --> 00:11:00,840
hook script. pre-commit run --all-files
is the documented CI usage.

149
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:05,031
A branch name as rev isn't
supported: pin a tag or a commit ID.

150
00:11:05,371 --> 00:11:10,681
The configuration is versioned and the tools
are pinned, so every engineer runs the

151
00:11:10,721 --> 00:11:15,194
same checks. That makes the framework
better than hand-copied scripts.

152
00:11:15,234 --> 00:11:20,609
It doesn't make it a control. pre-commit
install is still a step that each clone has

153
00:11:20,649 --> 00:11:25,380
to take. git commit --no-verify and
the framework's SKIP variable skip it.

154
00:11:25,420 --> 00:11:30,666
And each repo entry is third-party code
that runs on your machine and, in CI, next

155
00:11:30,706 --> 00:11:35,308
to your credentials. What enforces is
the CI run, required by a ruleset.

156
00:11:37,148 --> 00:11:38,796
Five mistakes to watch for.

157
00:11:39,136 --> 00:11:42,363
"The hook is in the repository,
so everyone has it."

158
00:11:42,403 --> 00:11:47,515
Root cause: a tracked hooks directory
travels, but core.hooksPath is configuration

159
00:11:47,555 --> 00:11:49,525
and each clone must set it once.

160
00:11:49,865 --> 00:11:54,997
Treating a client-side hook as a control.
Root cause: it fails open in four ways:

161
00:11:55,037 --> 00:12:00,361
not installed by cloning, skipped by one
option, absent for web and API commits, and

162
00:12:00,401 --> 00:12:03,170
not run by several
commit-creating commands.

163
00:12:03,510 --> 00:12:07,165
Running Git commands in an unpacked
archive that contains .git.

164
00:12:07,205 --> 00:12:12,032
Root cause: its configuration and hooks
came from the archive's author and run with

165
00:12:12,072 --> 00:12:14,437
your permissions; a clone copies neither.

166
00:12:14,777 --> 00:12:19,368
Assuming core.hooksPath=/dev/null
disables everything.

167
00:12:19,408 --> 00:12:26,572
Root cause: on Git 2.55.0 it silenced the
hooks directory and configured hooks still

168
00:12:26,612 --> 00:12:26,915
ran.

169
00:12:27,255 --> 00:12:30,214
A hook that reformats files
and commits the result.

170
00:12:30,254 --> 00:12:34,977
Root cause: it either changes the working
tree and not the index, or runs git add

171
00:12:35,017 --> 00:12:39,682
and commits content you did not review;
prefer hooks that refuse and say what to

172
00:12:39,722 --> 00:12:39,917
run.

173
00:12:41,757 --> 00:12:46,447
Now, out of the lab. After the secret
reached main, the team writes a one-page

174
00:12:46,487 --> 00:12:47,902
placement of its checks.

175
00:12:48,242 --> 00:12:53,116
Secrets: a pre-commit hook for the fast
warning, push protection on the server, and

176
00:12:53,156 --> 00:12:57,911
rotation of the leaked key first of all,
because blocking the next secret doesn't

177
00:12:57,951 --> 00:13:02,884
remove this one. Unfinished commits on main:
a pre-push guard for convenience, and a

178
00:13:02,924 --> 00:13:07,324
ruleset that requires pull requests.
Formatting and linting: the pre-commit

179
00:13:07,364 --> 00:13:12,237
framework locally, with pinned revisions,
and the same configuration run in CI as a

180
00:13:12,277 --> 00:13:17,151
required check. Notebook outputs and large
files: the hook, and a CI job that fails

181
00:13:17,191 --> 00:13:18,927
when the stored form is wrong.

182
00:13:19,267 --> 00:13:24,115
In every row the hook is in the first column
and something the author can't skip is

183
00:13:24,155 --> 00:13:29,063
in the second. The page also says who reviews
changes to the shared hooks directory:

184
00:13:29,103 --> 00:13:33,892
the same people who review CI configuration,
because both are programs that run on

185
00:13:33,932 --> 00:13:35,306
other people's machines.

